This file contains more detailed doctests for the stdnum.ir.nid module.

>>> from stdnum.ir import nid
>>> from stdnum.exceptions import *


Numbers that are published as examples of valid national ID numbers.

>>> numbers = '''
...
... 0499370899
... 0084575948
... 0067749828
... 0012345679
...
... '''
>>> [x for x in numbers.splitlines() if x and not nid.is_valid(x)]
[]


Leading zeros are often lost when the number is stored as an integer, so
shorter numbers are padded.

>>> nid.compact('84575948')
'0084575948'
>>> nid.validate('84575948')
'0084575948'
>>> nid.validate('12345679')
'0012345679'
>>> nid.validate('1234567')  # too short
Traceback (most recent call last):
    ...
InvalidLength: ...
>>> nid.validate('04993708990')  # too long
Traceback (most recent call last):
    ...
InvalidLength: ...


Separators and surrounding whitespace are removed.

>>> nid.validate(' 049-937089-9 ')
'0499370899'


Numbers consisting of a single repeated digit are not valid, even though some
of them pass the checksum.

>>> nid.is_valid('0000000000')
False
>>> nid.is_valid('1111111111')
False
>>> nid.is_valid('2222222222')
False
>>> nid.validate('1111111111')
Traceback (most recent call last):
    ...
InvalidFormat: ...


Other invalid numbers.

>>> nid.validate('0499370898')  # wrong check digit
Traceback (most recent call last):
    ...
InvalidChecksum: ...
>>> nid.validate('04993708A9')  # not numeric
Traceback (most recent call last):
    ...
InvalidFormat: ...
>>> nid.validate('\u0660\u0664\u0669\u0669\u0663\u0667\u0660\u0668\u0669\u0669')  # non-ASCII digits
Traceback (most recent call last):
    ...
InvalidFormat: ...
>>> nid.validate('')
Traceback (most recent call last):
    ...
InvalidFormat: ...
>>> nid.is_valid('0499370898')
False


Check digit calculation.

>>> nid.calc_check_digits('049937089')
'9'
>>> nid.calc_check_digits('008457594')
'8'
>>> nid.calc_check_digits('000000000')
'0'
